obj={'schema_version':1,'title':'Stress selection for few-test fatigue transfer: a source-audited first-exposure benchmark for LPBF316L','subtitle':'Exploratory working paper — current evidence through20October2026','date':'2026-10-20','status':'Unsubmitted draft. One fully lineaged additional RT author campaign; second independent matched campaign unresolved.','authorship':'[Author name and affiliation to be confirmed by the human author]','abstract':'''Few-test adaptation can lengthen proposed fatigue-life bounds while leaving their reliability unresolved. We recover valid virgin first loading blocks from a public stepped-load316L campaign by joining author specimen coordinates to thesis build and powder records, and compare stress acquisition on fixed common queries. Of70 physically unique initial blocks,43 use nominal stress in air at20°C;31 specimens in3 conditions from2 builds enter acquisition, all within one author campaign. Four policies and six right-censored models use10 deterministic query splits and feasible support budgets k3/5. The frozen10^7-cycle planning scenario and10^6 sensitivity preserve actual earlier censor stops. At k3, high-stress selection versus random increases proposed lower-life utility by1.336× for hierarchical Basquin and1.085× for normal-normal RFL, while reducing observed support cycles by7.5%. Paired censoring identification still permits risk increases of26.11 and6.67 percentage points. One campaign cannot provide campaign-bootstrap confidence intervals, so reliability/usefulness gates are not evaluable. A separate specimen-only panel shows model-dependent policy reversals. Independent arithmetic,19 synthetic tests and clean replay verify execution, not physical reliability. The contribution is an auditable source-recovery and evaluation case study; no general90% design guarantee, new optimal policy, original-BNN superiority or completed multi-campaign confirmation is established.''','keywords':['fatigue','LPBF316L','right censoring','random fatigue limit','few-test transfer','stress selection','physical provenance'],'sections':sections,'tables':tables,'references':[{'id':i,'citation':c,'doi':d,'url':('https://doi.org/'+d if d else 'https://pastel.hal.science/tel-03688021v1'),'evidence_scope':e} for i,c,d,e in refs],'declarations':['No correspondence, external upload, publication or new physical testing was performed.','No original BNN/Owen reproduction or allegation of error in unavailable original data is made.','Population parameter uncertainty is omitted; no conformal or R90C90 guarantee is claimed.','Source recovery and all reported policies are exploratory; public outcomes had already been seen.'],'render_notes':{'target_pages':'6–8','tables':'T1 may be moved to supplement if needed, but retain all6 models/4 policies in the released CSVs. T2 is central.','source_references':'Cite [numbers] near the relevant claims. Evidence_scope records access limits and should be retained in the supplement or source note.','current_snapshot':'Do not incorporate an unverified pending Solberg lead as data; update only after source audit and a separately frozen protocol if warranted.'}}
